    "body": "EICMA 2026 • MTPeak Stole Some of the Spotlight at EICMA 2026 With Insane Power and Retro Looks on Three Wheels\nStellantis Finally Sells Idled Brampton Plant, Canadian Defense Specialist Roshel Acquired It\n7 photos\nPhoto: Stellantis\nStellantis has found a buyer for its Brampton vehicle assembly plant. While that may seem like good news, please do not confuse a return to manufacturing with a return to automotive production on the same scale. Canadian defense specialist Roshel wants to turn the former Chrysler factory into an advanced defense manufacturing center, potentially bringing hundreds of workers back.\nReplacing a mass-production car factory with a defense contractor doesn't automatically restore historical production volumes, the previous headcount, or the supplier business that Brampton supported when it produced Dodge and Chrysler models there. Stellantis and Roshel have recently signed an asset purchase agreement covering the facility and its associated equipment.\nNeither party has disclosed the purchase price, and the transaction does require government and regulatory approval. If everything goes according to plan, it should be completed by the end of 2026. Stellantis and Roshel are further exploring cooperation involving vehicle technologies and services tailored for the defense industry.\nThat being said, Brampton hasn't assembled a single road-going vehicle since December 2023. Its last hurrah included three familiar nameplates, beginning with the Chrysler 300 and mechanically similar Dodge Charger. The Challenger was also made there, for all three models featured mostly similar underpinnings and oily bits.\nThe final 300 rolled off the line on December 20 three years ago, whereas the last Charger and Challenger followed two days later. According to Dodge, the final units were a Destroyer Gray Scat Pack Widebody Charger and a Pitch Black SRT Demon 170 Challenger. The 300 had its own farewell earlier that month, with Chrysler bidding farewell to the full-size luxury sedan on December 8, 2023.\nPhoto: Stellantis That final car was a HEMI-powered 300C finished in Velvet Red. Following these models, Brampton was supposed to have another Stellantis automotive chapter in the form of the Jeep Compass. The multinational automaker suspended the program in February 2025, moving Compass production to Belvidere in Illinois.\nUnfortunately, this change of plans resulted in a Brampton workforce that remained on extended layoff. The prospect of a new vehicle program gradually disappeared, affecting nearby automotive suppliers in the process. Now that Roshel is calling the shots, the Canadian defense company needs additional manufacturing space.\nChief executive officer Roman Shimonov made it clear that the company had outgrown its existing manufacturing and research facilities, with Shimonov also highlighting that Roshel had produced more than 2,500 vehicles for Ukraine alone. Meaningful business, for sure, and it's even more so for a relatively young company. More specifically, Roshel Defence Solutions was founded in Toronto a decade ago, back in 2016.\nA passenger vehicle assembly plant, however, depends on sustained production volumes and large workforces. Defense manufacturers may also need complex supply chains and skilled workers, but their output revolves around government or institutional procurement contracts. Otherwise put, Roshel isn't going to operate anything resembling the production system of Stellantis at the Brampton plant.\nSpeaking of Stellantis, the defense specialist intends to lease a quarter of the property back to Stellantis for automotive work. What kind of work? According to The Globe and Mail, that would be \"possible vehicle assembly.\" It's easy to imagine a military version of the Ram HD or the Jeep Gladiator, but so far, nothing has been confirmed by either Stellantis or Roshel in this regard.\nVia // The Globe and Mail\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nStellantis roshel production industry Canada\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT He Bought a Tesla Model X Plaid in March, Already Got Rid of It and Will Buy a Ford F-150 Lightning",
